0120:     """Complete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.
0121: 
0122:     Input structure
0123:     - The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.
0124:     - The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.
0125: 
0126:     Rule
0127:     - A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.
0128:     - A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.
0129:     - Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.
0130: 
0131:     Key insight
0132:     - The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.
0133:     """
0134:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
0135:     bg = get_bg(g)
0136:     colors = colors_present(g)
0137:     assert len(colors) == 2, "task expects exactly one foreground color and one background"
